---
/**
 * The privacy page (#422; site redesign spec §4.5, #454): what Google Analytics 4 collects on
 * genome-os.org, which cookies it sets and when (opt-in in the EEA, the UK, Switzerland, Quebec
 * and Turkey; opt-out elsewhere, with Global Privacy Control honoured), how long they and the data
 * last, and how to change the choice.
 *
 * It opens on the "What leaves your browser" figure, drawn in the visitor's own consent state, and
 * then lists every disclosure in a two-column ledger (term | detail). The figure's chips carry
 * the lists, so the ledger says each thing once. Every build publishes the page; a build without
 * analytics (the /genomeOS/ fallback, local dev) says so first and draws the gate removed.
 */
import BrandName from '../components/BrandName.astro';
import CookieSettings from '../components/CookieSettings.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import PrivacyFlow from '../components/privacy/PrivacyFlow.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { gaMeasurementId } from '../lib/ga.mjs';
import '../styles/pages/privacy.css';

const analytics = gaMeasurementId() !== null;
const external = { target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener noreferrer' };
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="How genome-os.org uses Google Analytics, which cookies it sets and when, and how to change your choice."
>
  <div class="privacy-lead" data-concept="what-leaves-your-browser">
    <PageIntro
      layout="split"
      note="Privacy"
      title="What we measure, and what we don't."
      lede="Google Analytics shows us which pages and Atlas datasets people use, so we can improve them. No ads, and never what you view on the map."
    >
      <PrivacyFlow slot="figure" />
    </PageIntro>
  </div>

  <section class="disclosure" aria-labelledby="disclosure-title">
    <div class="section-shell disclosure__inner">
      <h2 class="visually-hidden" id="disclosure-title">The disclosures</h2>
      {
        !analytics && (
          <article
            data-no-analytics
            class="disclosure__row disclosure__row--notice"
          >
            <p class="disclosure__term">This copy of the site</p>
            <p class="disclosure__detail">
              This copy of <BrandName /> loads no analytics and sets no cookies.
              The rest of this page describes genome-os.org, which does.
            </p>
          </article>
        )
      }
      <dl class="disclosure__list">
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Cookies</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            Google Analytics 4 sets two first-party cookies, <span
              class="disclosure__name">_ga</span
            > and <span class="disclosure__name">_ga_&lt;container&gt;</span>,
            which tell one visit from the next. Each lasts up to two years
            unless you turn analytics off.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Who is asked first</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            <strong
              >European Economic Area, UK, Switzerland, Quebec and Turkey:
              opt-in.</strong
            >{' '}
            This includes the EU’s overseas regions, Åland, the Channel Islands, the
            Isle of Man and Gibraltar. Devices set to Eastern Time in Canada, which
            covers Ontario as well as most of Quebec, are asked too. Until you choose{
              ' '
            }
            <strong>Accept</strong>, the site doesn’t load Google Analytics, so
            nothing is sent to Google. Visitors in these time zones see a short
            Accept or Decline prompt on their first visit.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Cookieless pings</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            If Google places you in these regions but your device is set to a
            time zone elsewhere, Google Analytics loads without setting cookies
            and sends cookieless pings, which Google uses only to estimate
            totals, until you choose.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Everywhere else</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            <strong>Everywhere else: opt-out.</strong>{' '}
            The cookies are set by default unless you turn them off, or your browser
            sends a <a href="https://globalprivacycontrol.org/" {...external}
              >Global Privacy Control</a
            >{' '}
            signal, which we honour as an opt-out.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Where your choice lives</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            Your choice is kept in your browser’s local storage, not in a
            cookie, on every page until you change it. While analytics is off,
            pages don’t load Google Analytics, and turning it off removes its
            cookies.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Collected</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            Only what the figure shows. Paths and linking pages lose their
            query, and your approximate location (city, region, country) comes
            from your IP address; Google Analytics 4 does not log or store IP
            addresses.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Atlas actions</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            Three, by public name only: which dataset is opened (only once you
            have chosen <strong>Accept</strong> or turned analytics on yourself, wherever
            you are, because a dataset can hint at a health interest); switches between
            globe, map and perspective views; and which inspector is opened. Never
            the places you look at or click.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">How long</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            Google Analytics keeps this visit-level data for two months, then
            deletes it; only aggregate reports are kept.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Not collected</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            No advertising or ad personalisation. Google signals are off, so
            visits are not linked to Google accounts or across devices. No names
            or accounts.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Map imagery</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            Map imagery and the Atlas’s optional data lookups load straight from
            their providers, such as OpenStreetMap and Cesium ion, under their
            own policies.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row" id="your-choice">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Change your choice at any time</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            <p>
              Every page has a small cookie icon in a bottom corner. In the
              Atlas it sits under the map controls on a wide screen, and at the
              end of the data credits on a phone. It opens your cookie settings,
              with a switch for analytics cookies. The footer’s <strong
                >Cookie settings</strong
              > link opens the same panel. Blocking Google Analytics in your browser
              or content blocker also stops it; the site works the same without it.
            </p>
            {
              analytics && (
                <p class="disclosure__action">
                  <CookieSettings class="privacy-cookie-settings" />
                </p>
              )
            }
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="disclosure__row">
          <dt class="disclosure__term">Who runs it</dt>
          <dd class="disclosure__detail">
            Google runs the service, and the <BrandName /> maintainers see only its
            aggregate reports. Google’s <a
              href="https://policies.google.com/privacy"
              {...external}>privacy policy</a
            > and its page on <a
              href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites"
              {...external}
              >how Google uses information from sites that use its services</a
            > apply to it.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
